---
# THE SITE AS A VISITOR SEES IT: blackbox_exporter on monitoring-1 fetching the public URLs over
# the internet, through Cloudflare (nix/modules/roles/synthetic-probes.nix).
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS WHEN web-errors.rules ALREADY DOES. Every web alert reads a counter the
# application increments, so it can only see a request that REACHED a pod. An expired origin
# certificate, a Caddy vhost that stopped matching, a Cloudflare rule that challenges real
# browsers, a country prefix routed to the wrong NodePort, the node itself unreachable: on each,
# nothing arrives to be counted, every web counter stays clean, and the site is down. These rules
# are the only ones here that ask the question from the outside.
#
# THE TARGETS carry `country` (or `all` for showtimes.cc's picker) and `kind`: `front-door`,
# `country-root`, `city`, and the discovered `film` and `share-card`. `instance` is the URL.
#
# NOT REPLAYED AGAINST HISTORY, because there is none: the exporter is new with this file. The
# thresholds are argued below, and the first fortnight of `probe_duration_seconds` is what to check
# them against.
#
# A 403 IS CLOUDFLARE REFUSING THE PROBE, not the site failing a visitor: Bot Fight Mode challenges
# a Hetzner address until monitoring-1 has an IP Access Rule, and showtimes.cc challenges every
# `/movie/` path for anything that is not a verified bot. `ProbeBlockedByEdge` reports it (a warning
# naming the fix) and ProbeFailing excludes it. The `/movie/` challenge is permanent and blackbox
# cannot pass it, so the discovery does not probe such a film page at all -- it reads it from the
# origin to find the share card, and probes that (synthetic-probe-targets.sh).
#
# LAST_OVER_TIME, NOT THE RAW SERIES. City pages are probed every five minutes (roles/synthetic-
# probes.nix), the length of Prometheus's lookback, so a raw read finds NOTHING whenever the next
# probe lands a moment late -- and every such gap restarts a `for` hold. Six minutes bridges one
# five-minute probe; for the one-minute probes it is just the latest sample.
groups:
  - name: synthetic-probes
    rules:
      # FIVE MINUTES: five one-minute probes failing in a row (two, for a city page probed every
      # five). One failed fetch is the internet; five is the site, or the path to it. Short, because this is the alert for "a visitor gets
      # nothing", and critical for the same reason.
      #
      # A 403 IS NOT THIS ALERT. Cloudflare's Bot Fight Mode answers every Hetzner address with a
      # 403 challenge unless monitoring-1 has an IP Access Rule, so without it every probe fails
      # -- and that must not page. `ProbeBlockedByEdge` below is the 403 case.
      - alert: ProbeFailing
        expr: >-
          last_over_time(probe_success[6m]) == 0
            unless on (instance) last_over_time(probe_http_status_code[6m]) == 403
        for: 5m
        labels:
          severity: critical
        annotations:
          summary: "{{ $labels.instance }} has failed from outside for 5 minutes"
          description: >-
            blackbox_exporter on monitoring-1 has been unable to fetch {{ $labels.instance }}
            ({{ $labels.kind }}, {{ $labels.country }}) through Cloudflare for five minutes.
            `probe_http_status_code` for this instance says which kind (403 is excluded here --
            that is ProbeBlockedByEdge): 5xx is the origin (Caddy or the pod -- check `WebServerErrorsServed` and the pod's
            readiness); 0 is no HTTP answer at all (DNS, TLS, or the node unreachable --
            `probe_failed_due_to_regex` 1 means a 200 that stopped short of `</html>`). A `film` or
            `share-card` target that 404s alone is a film that left the schedule and a discovery
            that has not replaced it -- `ProbeDiscoveryStale` fires alongside if so.

      # CLOUDFLARE REFUSED THE PROBE. A WARNING WITH INSTRUCTIONS, NOT A PAGE: without the one-time
      # IP Access Rule this is every probe, and it says nothing about whether visitors are served.
      # Fifteen minutes so one rate-limited minute does not message.
      - alert: ProbeBlockedByEdge
        expr: last_over_time(probe_http_status_code[6m]) == 403
        for: 15m
        labels:
          severity: warning
        annotations:
          summary: "Cloudflare is refusing the synthetic probe of {{ $labels.instance }} (403)"
          description: >-
            The probe from monitoring-1 got a 403 from Cloudflare -- so this URL is NOT being
            watched from outside, and ProbeFailing cannot fire for it. `curl -sI` it from
            monitoring-1: `cf-mitigated: challenge` on every URL is Bot Fight Mode challenging a
            Hetzner address, and the fix (one-time) is an IP Access Rule for 128.140.49.167
            (monitoring-1) with action Allow, in BOTH zones (kinowo.net and showtimes.cc),
            Security > WAF > Tools > IP Access Rules. On some URLs only, a custom or crawler rule
            is matching the path or the probe's User-Agent (`kinowo-synthetic-probe/1`): the
            showtimes.cc `/movie/` managed challenge is one, and the discovery already keeps film
            pages under it out of the probes, so a film page here means that rule has spread. If
            real visitors report the same 403, this is an outage, not a probe problem.

      # FOUR SECONDS, AVERAGED OVER TEN MINUTES AND HELD TEN MORE. The same pages answer in
      # 0.2-0.5s from a laptop and from the fleet's own egress (measured 2026-09-24, 184KB film
      # page included), so four seconds is eight times the normal path and well short of the 12s
      # probe timeout -- slow enough to be a real degradation a visitor feels, not a slow minute.
      # Successful probes only: a failing one is ProbeFailing's, and its timeout would otherwise
      # read as slowness.
      - alert: ProbeSlow
        expr: >-
          avg_over_time(probe_duration_seconds[10m]) > 4
            and on (instance) last_over_time(probe_success[6m]) == 1
        for: 10m
        labels:
          severity: warning
        annotations:
          summary: "{{ $labels.instance }} is taking {{ $value | humanize }}s to fetch from outside"
          description: >-
            {{ $labels.instance }} ({{ $labels.kind }}, {{ $labels.country }}) answers, but has
            averaged over four seconds for twenty minutes against a normal half second.
            `probe_http_duration_seconds` splits the time by phase: `connect`/`tls` is the path to
            Cloudflare, `processing` is the origin. If processing dominates, `WebPageLatencyHigh`
            should agree from inside; if it does not, the time is going between Cloudflare and
            Caddy.

      # THE FILM AND SHARE-CARD TARGETS ARE ONLY AS GOOD AS THEIR DISCOVERY. It runs every two
      # minutes and publishes a timestamp only when it found a live film for every country; fifteen
      # minutes past the last success, held fifteen more, is half an hour -- fourteen runs in a row
      # that did not: a city page that lists no films, or one that stopped answering. While it lasts, the film probes point at whatever was found
      # last, which will 404 as soon as that film leaves the schedule.
      - alert: ProbeDiscoveryStale
        expr: >-
          (time() - kinowo_synthetic_probe_discovery_last_success_timestamp_seconds > 15 * 60)
            or absent(kinowo_synthetic_probe_discovery_last_success_timestamp_seconds)
        for: 15m
        labels:
          severity: warning
        annotations:
          summary: "The synthetic probes have not found a live film page for every country in 30 minutes"
          description: >-
            `synthetic-probe-targets` on monitoring-1 has not completed a discovery, so the `film`
            and `share-card` probes are aimed at pages found earlier (or at none, if it has never
            run). `journalctl -u synthetic-probe-targets -n 50` names the country and the reason:
            a city page that did not answer, or one that lists no film. An ABSENT timestamp means
            it has never succeeded on this host -- most likely the unit was never switched in, or
            Cloudflare is still challenging monitoring-1.

      - alert: ProbeResultsAbsent
        expr: absent(probe_success)
        # THIRTY MINUTES, matching every other `absent()` companion in this directory.
        for: 30m
        labels:
          severity: warning
        annotations:
          summary: "Nothing is reporting the synthetic probes"
          description: >-
            `ProbeFailing` is a comparison and cannot fire without `probe_success`, so the site is
            unwatched from outside. Either the `blackbox` scrape job is missing from
            /etc/prometheus/scrape.d/ (`fleet.syntheticProbes.enable` off, or the host never
            switched to the closure that adds it), or blackbox_exporter is down -- in which case
            `TargetDown` for job `blackbox` says so first. IT DOES NOT MEAN THE SITE IS UP.
